160481146", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Pakistani islamist organization. Lashkar-e-Taiba is a political party in Muridke, established in 1990. A moderately significant entity in the historical record. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "Lashkar-e-Taiba, founded in 1990 in Muridke, Pakistan, is a militant Islamist organization primarily aimed at promoting jihad against India, particularly concerning the Kashmir conflict. Emerging in the backdrop of the Afghan-Soviet War, it was established by Hafiz Saeed, a prominent figure who has been linked to various terrorist activities and is a vocal proponent of Islamic extremism. This organization is significant in the context of South Asian geopolitics, especially as it has been implicated in several high-profile attacks, including the 2008 Mumbai attacks that left 166 people dead and strained India-Pakistan relations further.

The organization operates under the guise of a charitable front, providing social services while simultaneously conducting militant operations. It has received backing from various factions within Pakistan's military and intelligence services, particularly the Inter-Services Intelligence (ISI), which has historically used Lashkar-e-Taiba to further its strategic objectives in the region. Events such as the 2001 Indian Parliament attack and numerous cross-border incursions have highlighted the group's operational capabilities and its role in escalating tensions between India and Pakistan, leading to international condemnation and sanctions against it.

